---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import RowImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/1-16-row.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const TheHardWay = `local count = 1

while count <= 8 do
    print(count)
    count = count + 1
end`;

const TheShortWay = `for i = 1, 8 do
    print(i)
end`;

const CountOut = `1
2
3
4
5
6
7
8`;

const Step = `for i = 1, 10, 3 do
    print(i)
end`;

const StepOut = `1
4
7
10`;

const Backwards = `for i = 5, 1, -1 do
    print(i)
end`;

const BackwardsOut = `5
4
3
2
1`;

const Scope = `for i = 1, 3 do
    print("inside " .. i)
end

print("after: " .. tostring(i))`;

const ScopeOut = `inside 1
inside 2
inside 3
after: nil`;

const Row = `local count = 8
local gap = 800 / (count + 1)

function love.draw()
    for i = 1, count do
        love.graphics.circle("fill", i * gap, 300, 34)
    end
end`;

const Colored = `for i = 1, count do
    love.graphics.setColor(i / count, 0.4, 1 - i / count)
    love.graphics.circle("fill", i * gap, 300, 34)
end`;

const NotationLua = `for i = 1, 8 do
    print(i)
end`;

const NotationPseudo = `i ← 1

REPEAT 8 TIMES
{
  DISPLAY(i)
  i ← i + 1
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.16: Counting Loops</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.16</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Counting Loops</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>A Loop That Counts For You</p>
              <p>Using the Counter</p>
              <p>Drawing N Things</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What You Are Building</h2>
              <Image
                src={RowImage}
                alt="Eight circles in a row across a black window, shading from blue on the left to orange on the right"
                width={520}
                class="img-fluid rounded"
              />
              <p class="mt-3">
                Eight circles from{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">one circle command.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Last Session, the Long Way</h2>
              <Code code={TheHardWay} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Last Session, the Long Way</h2>
              <Code code={TheHardWay} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                Start it, check it, change it.{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Three lines of bookkeeping for one
                line of work.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Today, the Short Way</h2>
              <Code code={TheShortWay} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Today, the Short Way</h2>
              <Code code={TheShortWay} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <Code code={CountOut} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                All three parts are on the first line.{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">You cannot forget the one that
                changes.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Reading the First Line</h2>
              <Code
                code={`for i = 1, 8 do`}
                frame="code"
                lang="lua"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>i</dt>
                  <dd>A variable the loop makes for you. You pick the name.</dd>
                  <dt>1</dt>
                  <dd>What it starts at.</dd>
                  <dt>8</dt>
                  <dd>What it counts up to, <b>and including</b>.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Eight passes, and <code>i</code> is 8 on the last one. Not 7.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>A Third Number Changes the Step</h2>
              <Code code={Step} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>A Third Number Changes the Step</h2>
              <Code code={Step} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <Code code={StepOut} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                It stopped at 10 because 13 would be past the end.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>And a Negative Step Goes Backwards</h2>
              <Code code={Backwards} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>And a Negative Step Goes Backwards</h2>
              <Code code={Backwards} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <Code code={BackwardsOut} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                Which is last session's countdown,{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">in one line.</b>
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Counter Is the Useful Part</h2>
              <p>
                <code>i</code> is not just bookkeeping. It is{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">a different number on every pass</b>,
                and you can put it anywhere a number goes.
              </p>
              <p class="text-body-secondary">
                Which is how one <code>circle</code> line draws eight circles in
                eight places.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>i Only Exists Inside</h2>
              <Code code={Scope} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>i Only Exists Inside</h2>
              <Code code={Scope} frame="code" lang="lua" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <Code code={ScopeOut} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p>
                The loop made it, used it, and{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">threw it away.</b> Outside, it is{" "}
                <code>nil</code>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Write a counting loop</li>
                <li>Use the counter inside the loop</li>
                <li>Change the start, the end, and the step</li>
                <li>Draw N shapes from one drawing command</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>for</dt>
                  <dd>A loop that counts, with all three parts on one line.</dd>
                  <dt>Loop counter</dt>
                  <dd>The variable the loop makes and changes for you.</dd>
                  <dt>Step</dt>
                  <dd>How much the counter changes each pass. One unless you say otherwise.</dd>
                  <dt>Scope</dt>
                  <dd>Where a name exists. The counter exists only inside its loop.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 1: Count in the Terminal</h3>

          <p>
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-console" lang="txt" inline /> to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1-16-count" lang="txt" inline />. Predict the
            output of each of these <b>before</b> running it, and write both
            your prediction and the result:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={`for i = 1, 5 do
    print(i)
end

for i = 3, 7 do
    print(i)
end

for i = 1, 10, 2 do
    print(i)
end

for i = 10, 1, -2 do
    print(i)
end`}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
          />

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The third and fourth are the ones people
            get wrong.</b> Count how many lines each produces before you run it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 2: Use the Counter</h3>

          <p>Add these two, and work out what they do first:</p>

          <Code
            code={`for i = 1, 10 do
    print(i .. " times 7 is " .. i * 7)
end

for i = 1, 5 do
    print(i .. " squared is " .. i * i)
end`}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
          />

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            A times table in three lines. Change the 7 and you have a different
            one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 3: Eight Circles, One Command</h3>

          <p>
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> to{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="1-16-row" lang="txt" inline />:
          </p>

          <Code code={Row} lang="lua" frame="code" title="main.lua" mark={{ range: "5-7" }} />

          <p>
            Run it, then <b class="text-sn-theme">change <code>count</code> to
            3, then to 20.</b> The circles space themselves out, because{" "}
            <code>gap</code> is worked out from <code>count</code> rather than
            typed in.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Why <CodeSnippet code="count + 1" lang="lua" inline />?</b> With
            8 circles there are 9 gaps &mdash; one before the first and one
            after the last. Try <CodeSnippet code="800 / count" lang="lua" inline />{" "}
            instead and see where the last circle ends up.
          </div>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 4: Make the Counter Visible</h3>

          <p>Use <code>i</code> for the color as well as the position:</p>

          <Code code={Colored} lang="lua" frame="code" title="main.lua" mark={{ range: "2" }} />

          <Image
            src={RowImage}
            alt="Eight circles shading smoothly from blue on the left to orange on the right"
            width={520}
            class="img-fluid rounded my-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Every circle is drawn by the same line of code</b>, and no two
            look the same. Write down what <code>i / count</code> is on the
            first pass and on the last.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Task 5: Your Own Pattern</h3>

          <p>
            Change the row into something of your own. Pick at least{" "}
            <b>two</b>:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Make the circles grow as they go across, using <code>i</code> for the radius</li>
            <li>Make them into a diagonal line instead of a row</li>
            <li>Draw squares, and rotate the colors the other way</li>
            <li>Use a negative step so the loop draws right to left, and explain why the picture is identical</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Challenge (Optional): Break It on Purpose</h3>

          <p>Move the drawing line outside the loop:</p>

          <Code
            code={`for i = 1, count do
end

love.graphics.circle("fill", i * gap, 300, 34)`}
            lang="lua"
            frame="code"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p>
            <b>The window turns blue.</b> Copy the message, then explain it
            using what the slides said about where <code>i</code> exists.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Challenge (Optional): A Circle of Circles</h3>

          <p>
            Arrange the circles in a ring instead of a row. You need{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="math.cos" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="math.sin" lang="lua" inline />, and the angle for
            circle <code>i</code> out of <code>count</code> is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="i / count * 2 * math.pi" lang="lua" inline />.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            This is a real use of trigonometry and you are not expected to
            derive it. Multiply the cosine by a radius, add 400, and see what
            happens.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Exam notation -->
        <section id="exam-notation">
          <h2>Same Idea, Exam Notation</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The exam has a counting loop, and it does
            not give you a counter.</b> This is the widest gap between Lua and
            the exam sheet in the whole quarter.
          </p>

          <div class="row g-3 my-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationLua}
                frame="code"
                lang="lua"
                title="Lua"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
              <Code
                code={NotationPseudo}
                frame="code"
                lang="txt"
                title="Exam Notation"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <h4>REPEAT n TIMES has no variable in it</h4>

          <p>
            <code>REPEAT 8 TIMES</code> runs a block eight times and{" "}
            <b>tells you nothing about which pass you are on</b>. If you need to
            know, you make your own counter, start it yourself, and add to it
            yourself &mdash; exactly the bookkeeping Lua's <code>for</code>{" "}
            removed.
          </p>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th></th>
                  <th>Lua <code>for</code></th>
                  <th><code>REPEAT n TIMES</code></th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Runs a fixed number of times</td>
                  <td>Yes</td>
                  <td>Yes</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Gives you a counter</td>
                  <td>Yes</td>
                  <td><b>No</b></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Can start somewhere other than 1</td>
                  <td>Yes</td>
                  <td>Only by hand</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Can step by something other than 1</td>
                  <td>Yes</td>
                  <td>Only by hand</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <div class="alert alert-warning">
            <b>There is no C-style <code>for</code> loop on the exam sheet at
            all.</b> Two loops, and that is the whole list:{" "}
            <code>REPEAT <i>n</i> TIMES</code> and{" "}
            <code>REPEAT UNTIL(condition)</code>.
          </div>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            On a trace question, the counter is usually the thing being asked
            about &mdash; so when you see <code>REPEAT <i>n</i> TIMES</code>,
            look for the variable somebody had to maintain by hand, and watch
            where it changes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>
              All four counting loops in task 1 have a prediction and a result
              written down
            </li>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet code="1-16-row" lang="txt" inline /> draws its
              circles from one <CodeSnippet code="circle" lang="lua" inline />{" "}
              line inside a loop
            </li>
            <li>
              Changing <code>count</code> alone changes how many there are and
              respaces them
            </li>
            <li>Two of the task 5 variations are done</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Last session's <CodeSnippet code="while" lang="lua" inline />{" "}
              loop needed three separate parts and this session's{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="for" lang="lua" inline /> puts all three on one
              line. Explain which kind of bug that makes impossible, and give an
              example of something <code>while</code> can still do that{" "}
              <code>for</code> cannot.
            </li>
            <li>
              Your eight circles were drawn by one line of code. Explain how one
              line put eight different shapes in eight different places.
            </li>
            <li>
              The exam's <code>REPEAT <i>n</i> TIMES</code> gives you no
              counter. Describe what you would have to add to it to draw your
              row of circles, and say how many extra lines that is.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
